1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,638
What if the most advanced chips on

2
00:00:02,638 --> 00:00:04,926
Earth could only be printed by one

3
00:00:04,926 --> 00:00:05,664
machine,

4
00:00:05,664 --> 00:00:08,206
and only one company knew how to

5
00:00:08,206 --> 00:00:09,314
build it?

6
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:11,820
That company exists.

7
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:14,882
Each machine costs about 250 million

8
00:00:14,882 --> 00:00:15,820
dollars.

9
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:17,747
And it comes from a small town

10
00:00:17,747 --> 00:00:19,551
in the Netherlands.

11
00:00:30,217 --> 00:00:33,397
What does this machine actually do?

12
00:00:33,587 --> 00:00:36,176
It matters because every phone,

13
00:00:36,176 --> 00:00:39,837
every laptop and every AI data center

14
00:00:39,837 --> 00:00:42,005
depends on what it prints.

15
00:00:42,250 --> 00:00:43,930
Let me draw it.

16
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:45,814
A chip is built by printing patterns

17
00:00:45,814 --> 00:00:48,058
onto a silicon wafer with light,

18
00:00:48,058 --> 00:00:50,100
a bit like a projector.

19
00:00:50,310 --> 00:00:52,109
Light shines through a mask with the

20
00:00:52,109 --> 00:00:53,211
circuit design,

21
00:00:53,211 --> 00:00:55,460
then lenses or mirrors shrink that image

22
00:00:55,460 --> 00:00:57,184
onto the wafer.

23
00:00:57,450 --> 00:00:58,337
The catch:

24
00:00:58,337 --> 00:01:00,831
you can't print details much smaller than

25
00:01:00,831 --> 00:01:02,540
the light you use.

26
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:04,893
And smaller details mean more transistors

27
00:01:04,893 --> 00:01:06,223
on every chip,

28
00:01:06,223 --> 00:01:07,327
which means faster,

29
00:01:07,327 --> 00:01:10,180
cheaper and more efficient computers.

30
00:01:10,430 --> 00:01:11,927
So to keep shrinking chips,

31
00:01:11,927 --> 00:01:14,096
the industry needed light with a much

32
00:01:14,096 --> 00:01:16,865
shorter wavelength than ever before.

33
00:01:17,117 --> 00:01:19,983
ASML's answer is extreme ultraviolet

34
00:01:19,983 --> 00:01:20,528
light,

35
00:01:20,528 --> 00:01:26,087
with a wavelength of just 13.5 nanometers.

36
00:01:26,397 --> 00:01:28,927
Nothing on Earth makes it naturally.

37
00:01:29,107 --> 00:01:31,387
So the machine makes its own.

38
00:01:31,607 --> 00:01:33,830
It fires tiny drops of molten tin,

39
00:01:33,830 --> 00:01:37,897
up to 50,000 of them every second.

40
00:01:38,067 --> 00:01:39,897
A laser flattens each drop,

41
00:01:39,897 --> 00:01:41,033
and a second,

42
00:01:41,033 --> 00:01:42,631
far more powerful pulse,

43
00:01:42,631 --> 00:01:44,925
hotter than the surface of the sun,

44
00:01:44,925 --> 00:01:47,242
turns it into a plasma that gives

45
00:01:47,242 --> 00:01:48,537
off the light.

46
00:01:48,897 --> 00:01:51,072
A special mirror collects that light and

47
00:01:51,072 --> 00:01:52,812
sends it into the rest of the

48
00:01:52,812 --> 00:01:53,847
machine.

49
00:01:54,007 --> 00:01:56,439
All of this happens again and again,

50
00:01:56,439 --> 00:01:57,756
every second,

51
00:01:57,756 --> 00:01:58,403
for hours,

52
00:01:58,403 --> 00:01:59,896
without stopping.

53
00:02:05,117 --> 00:02:07,413
But this light is absorbed by almost

54
00:02:07,413 --> 00:02:08,538
everything,

55
00:02:08,538 --> 00:02:09,827
even air.

56
00:02:10,037 --> 00:02:11,947
So the whole path runs in a

57
00:02:11,947 --> 00:02:12,639
vacuum,

58
00:02:12,639 --> 00:02:14,287
and instead of lenses,

59
00:02:14,287 --> 00:02:16,436
it bounces off a chain of ultra

60
00:02:16,436 --> 00:02:18,584
smooth mirrors before it reaches the

61
00:02:18,584 --> 00:02:19,662
wafer.

62
00:02:19,917 --> 00:02:22,671
The result is a machine the size

63
00:02:22,671 --> 00:02:24,170
of a school bus,

64
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:27,157
weighing 150 tons.

65
00:02:27,397 --> 00:02:28,973
To deliver one,

66
00:02:28,973 --> 00:02:32,230
ASML packs it into around 40 shipping

67
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:34,901
containers and flies it across the world

68
00:02:34,901 --> 00:02:36,836
on cargo planes.

69
00:02:40,917 --> 00:02:43,765
So why is there only one company

70
00:02:43,765 --> 00:02:45,504
that can build it?

71
00:02:51,150 --> 00:02:53,790
How did a small Dutch company end

72
00:02:53,790 --> 00:02:56,130
up as the only one?

73
00:02:56,130 --> 00:02:57,522
It matters because,

74
00:02:57,522 --> 00:02:59,151
in a normal market,

75
00:02:59,151 --> 00:03:01,639
monopolies like this don't last.

76
00:03:01,883 --> 00:03:04,763
The story starts in 1984,

77
00:03:04,763 --> 00:03:07,338
when the electronics giant Philips and a

78
00:03:07,338 --> 00:03:09,913
chip equipment maker called A S M

79
00:03:09,913 --> 00:03:12,957
International created a small new company

80
00:03:12,957 --> 00:03:14,383
near Eindhoven.

81
00:03:14,383 --> 00:03:17,283
A year later it moved to Veldhoven.

82
00:03:17,283 --> 00:03:18,784
Its early years were rough:

83
00:03:18,784 --> 00:03:19,388
a small,

84
00:03:19,388 --> 00:03:21,681
unknown newcomer in a market full of

85
00:03:21,681 --> 00:03:22,285
bigger,

86
00:03:22,285 --> 00:03:24,553
established competitors.

87
00:03:24,583 --> 00:03:26,425
In 1995,

88
00:03:26,425 --> 00:03:29,509
it became a fully independent company,

89
00:03:29,509 --> 00:03:31,706
listed in Amsterdam and New York,

90
00:03:31,706 --> 00:03:33,925
and it kept pouring money into one

91
00:03:33,925 --> 00:03:34,635
idea:

92
00:03:34,635 --> 00:03:37,010
printing with ever shorter light.

93
00:03:40,883 --> 00:03:43,826
The conflict came with extreme ultraviolet

94
00:03:43,826 --> 00:03:44,693
light.

95
00:03:44,803 --> 00:03:47,661
Making it work took decades and billions,

96
00:03:47,661 --> 00:03:50,663
and nobody was sure it ever would.

97
00:03:50,813 --> 00:03:52,396
So in twenty twelve,

98
00:03:52,396 --> 00:03:55,143
ASML did something unusual.

99
00:03:55,283 --> 00:03:57,269
Its own biggest customers,

100
00:03:57,269 --> 00:03:57,998
Intel,

101
00:03:57,998 --> 00:03:59,229
TSMC and Samsung,

102
00:03:59,229 --> 00:04:02,296
bought 23 percent of the company and

103
00:04:02,296 --> 00:04:04,655
helped pay for the research.

104
00:04:04,917 --> 00:04:05,684
For them,

105
00:04:05,684 --> 00:04:07,607
it was insurance.

106
00:04:07,797 --> 00:04:10,032
If the machine never arrived,

107
00:04:10,032 --> 00:04:11,977
their own roadmaps would stall.

108
00:04:11,977 --> 00:04:13,060
If it did,

109
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:15,325
they would be first in line.

110
00:04:15,583 --> 00:04:19,393
Then ASML went after the missing pieces.

111
00:04:19,403 --> 00:04:20,928
It moved to buy Cymer,

112
00:04:20,928 --> 00:04:23,226
the American maker of its light source,

113
00:04:23,226 --> 00:04:25,987
in a deal worth about 2.55 billion

114
00:04:25,987 --> 00:04:26,973
dollars.

115
00:04:27,123 --> 00:04:29,677
Later it took almost 25 percent of

116
00:04:29,677 --> 00:04:31,541
Carl Zeiss's chip optics business,

117
00:04:31,541 --> 00:04:34,653
the German company that makes its mirrors.

118
00:04:34,963 --> 00:04:36,733
In 2020,

119
00:04:36,733 --> 00:04:39,505
extreme ultraviolet machines finally went

120
00:04:39,505 --> 00:04:41,811
into high volume production.

121
00:04:42,050 --> 00:04:43,229
In the end,

122
00:04:43,229 --> 00:04:45,320
ASML didn't win alone.

123
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:47,452
It won by tying its customers and

124
00:04:47,452 --> 00:04:49,710
its suppliers to the same bet.

125
00:04:49,770 --> 00:04:51,946
Today it is the only company in

126
00:04:51,946 --> 00:04:54,122
the world that makes extreme ultraviolet

127
00:04:54,122 --> 00:04:54,894
machines,

128
00:04:54,894 --> 00:04:57,268
and it has become Europe's most valuable

129
00:04:57,268 --> 00:04:58,462
company.

130
00:04:58,717 --> 00:05:01,461
But a machine this complex is never

131
00:05:01,461 --> 00:05:03,707
built by one company.

132
00:05:03,987 --> 00:05:05,994
Who else is inside it?

133
00:05:11,650 --> 00:05:14,530
Who actually builds this machine?

134
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:17,435
It matters because the monopoly is not

135
00:05:17,435 --> 00:05:18,980
one company.

136
00:05:19,070 --> 00:05:21,170
It is a network.

137
00:05:21,417 --> 00:05:23,017
Let's open it up.

138
00:05:23,017 --> 00:05:24,143
At the bottom,

139
00:05:24,143 --> 00:05:25,980
the stage that holds the wafer and

140
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:29,077
moves it with incredible precision.

141
00:05:29,077 --> 00:05:29,873
Above it,

142
00:05:29,873 --> 00:05:31,527
the projection mirrors.

143
00:05:31,527 --> 00:05:33,987
Then the mask with the chip design.

144
00:05:33,987 --> 00:05:36,417
Then the optics that shape the light.

145
00:05:36,417 --> 00:05:37,832
And feeding it all,

146
00:05:37,832 --> 00:05:38,767
the light source,

147
00:05:38,767 --> 00:05:41,285
where the tin turns into plasma.

148
00:05:41,550 --> 00:05:44,700
Now follow the parts on a map.

149
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:46,362
The mirrors come from Zeiss,

150
00:05:46,362 --> 00:05:48,390
in southern Germany.

151
00:05:48,550 --> 00:05:51,603
ASML bought about 5 billion dollars of

152
00:05:51,603 --> 00:05:54,195
parts from Zeiss in twenty twenty-five

153
00:05:54,195 --> 00:05:55,030
alone,

154
00:05:55,030 --> 00:05:57,622
and Zeiss is its only supplier for

155
00:05:57,622 --> 00:05:58,510
them.

156
00:05:58,630 --> 00:05:59,421
In fact,

157
00:05:59,421 --> 00:06:02,421
Zeiss's sites in Oberkochen and Wetzlar

158
00:06:02,421 --> 00:06:05,149
are the only places where these optics

159
00:06:05,149 --> 00:06:06,213
can be built,

160
00:06:06,213 --> 00:06:10,032
so their capacity limits how many machines

161
00:06:10,032 --> 00:06:11,450
ASML can ship.

162
00:06:11,770 --> 00:06:13,551
The light source is developed by Cymer,

163
00:06:13,551 --> 00:06:14,330
in San Diego.

164
00:06:14,330 --> 00:06:15,442
In total,

165
00:06:15,442 --> 00:06:19,521
ASML works with about 5,100 suppliers,

166
00:06:19,521 --> 00:06:24,400
and employs more than 44,000 people.

167
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:27,839
Everything comes together in Veldhoven.

168
00:06:28,083 --> 00:06:30,743
Then comes the strangest part.

169
00:06:30,903 --> 00:06:32,966
Each machine is built and tested in

170
00:06:32,966 --> 00:06:34,090
the Netherlands,

171
00:06:34,090 --> 00:06:35,213
taken apart,

172
00:06:35,213 --> 00:06:36,795
packed into crates,

173
00:06:36,795 --> 00:06:38,606
flown to the customer's fab,

174
00:06:38,606 --> 00:06:40,123
and rebuilt there.

175
00:06:40,393 --> 00:06:43,381
Only then does it start printing chips.

176
00:06:48,083 --> 00:06:50,596
So where does all that money go,

177
00:06:50,596 --> 00:06:52,607
and how much is there?

178
00:06:58,250 --> 00:07:00,910
How does ASML make money?

179
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:03,515
It matters because this one company sits

180
00:07:03,515 --> 00:07:05,639
in the middle of the whole AI

181
00:07:05,639 --> 00:07:06,475
boom.

182
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:09,400
Let me draw the numbers.

183
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:11,103
In twenty twenty-five,

184
00:07:11,103 --> 00:07:14,113
ASML sold about 37 billion dollars worth

185
00:07:14,113 --> 00:07:16,310
of machines and services,

186
00:07:16,310 --> 00:07:19,180
up 16 percent in a year.

187
00:07:19,430 --> 00:07:21,827
It kept almost 11 billion dollars as

188
00:07:21,827 --> 00:07:22,820
profit.

189
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,431
Extreme ultraviolet was the star:

190
00:07:25,431 --> 00:07:27,123
48 machines,

191
00:07:27,123 --> 00:07:30,631
bringing in about 13 billion dollars.

192
00:07:30,883 --> 00:07:34,146
That is an average of roughly 270

193
00:07:34,146 --> 00:07:36,014
million dollars per machine,

194
00:07:36,014 --> 00:07:38,033
by our calculation.

195
00:07:38,383 --> 00:07:41,009
We show every figure in US dollars,

196
00:07:41,009 --> 00:07:43,878
converted at the official average exchange

197
00:07:43,878 --> 00:07:45,361
rate for the year.

198
00:07:45,617 --> 00:07:47,367
And who buys them?

199
00:07:47,517 --> 00:07:49,270
In twenty twenty-five,

200
00:07:49,270 --> 00:07:52,624
customers in China made up 29 percent

201
00:07:52,624 --> 00:07:53,869
of ASML's sales,

202
00:07:53,869 --> 00:07:56,384
none of it the newest machines,

203
00:07:56,384 --> 00:07:59,017
because China cannot buy them.

204
00:07:59,317 --> 00:08:02,467
A year earlier it was 36 percent.

205
00:08:02,507 --> 00:08:03,699
Then come Taiwan,

206
00:08:03,699 --> 00:08:06,351
South Korea and the United States,

207
00:08:06,351 --> 00:08:08,541
home to the chipmakers we met in

208
00:08:08,541 --> 00:08:10,357
the last episode.

209
00:08:10,617 --> 00:08:13,517
The money flows in a loop.

210
00:08:13,737 --> 00:08:16,237
Chipmakers pay for machines.

211
00:08:16,237 --> 00:08:20,099
ASML spends about 5.3 billion dollars a

212
00:08:20,099 --> 00:08:21,827
year on research.

213
00:08:22,167 --> 00:08:24,473
That research makes the next machine,

214
00:08:24,473 --> 00:08:26,779
which chipmakers need to stay ahead,

215
00:08:26,779 --> 00:08:28,777
so they order again.

216
00:08:29,167 --> 00:08:31,477
At the end of twenty twenty-five,

217
00:08:31,477 --> 00:08:34,789
ASML already had almost 44 billion dollars

218
00:08:34,789 --> 00:08:36,556
of orders waiting.

219
00:08:42,617 --> 00:08:45,420
A machine this valuable is also a

220
00:08:45,420 --> 00:08:46,530
weapon.

221
00:08:52,183 --> 00:08:54,156
Who decides where these machines are

222
00:08:54,156 --> 00:08:55,743
allowed to go?

223
00:08:55,873 --> 00:08:58,336
It matters because whoever controls the

224
00:08:58,336 --> 00:09:00,553
machine controls who can make the most

225
00:09:00,553 --> 00:09:02,139
advanced chips.

226
00:09:02,383 --> 00:09:05,287
Extreme ultraviolet machines flow to

227
00:09:05,287 --> 00:09:06,053
Taiwan,

228
00:09:06,053 --> 00:09:09,293
South Korea and the United States.

229
00:09:09,493 --> 00:09:11,293
But not to China.

230
00:09:11,373 --> 00:09:13,539
The Dutch government has not let ASML

231
00:09:13,539 --> 00:09:16,258
send them there since 2019,

232
00:09:16,258 --> 00:09:18,783
and it has never shipped one.

233
00:09:18,963 --> 00:09:20,728
In 2023,

234
00:09:20,728 --> 00:09:22,642
the Dutch government also began

235
00:09:22,642 --> 00:09:25,173
restricting some of the older machines,

236
00:09:25,173 --> 00:09:28,112
and extended the rules a year later.

237
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:30,300
Let me draw the gate.

238
00:09:30,300 --> 00:09:31,076
On one side,

239
00:09:31,076 --> 00:09:33,065
the supply network in Europe and the

240
00:09:33,065 --> 00:09:34,610
United States.

241
00:09:34,610 --> 00:09:35,711
On the other,

242
00:09:35,711 --> 00:09:36,750
the buyers.

243
00:09:36,750 --> 00:09:39,390
Each export license is a gate.

244
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:40,241
Open it,

245
00:09:40,241 --> 00:09:41,966
and a country gets the tools for

246
00:09:41,966 --> 00:09:43,790
the most advanced chips.

247
00:09:43,790 --> 00:09:44,568
Close it,

248
00:09:44,568 --> 00:09:46,447
and it has to try to build

249
00:09:46,447 --> 00:09:47,225
its own,

250
00:09:47,225 --> 00:09:49,435
which could take many years.

251
00:09:49,683 --> 00:09:52,492
The gate has a price for ASML

252
00:09:52,492 --> 00:09:53,493
too.

253
00:09:53,783 --> 00:09:55,913
The company expects China to fall to

254
00:09:55,913 --> 00:09:58,275
about 20 percent of its sales in

255
00:09:58,275 --> 00:09:59,843
twenty twenty-six.

256
00:10:00,153 --> 00:10:02,079
And China is now racing to build

257
00:10:02,079 --> 00:10:04,198
its own lithography machines,

258
00:10:04,198 --> 00:10:06,338
while the next generation of ASML's

259
00:10:06,338 --> 00:10:09,720
machines is estimated to cost 300 to

260
00:10:09,720 --> 00:10:12,203
400 million dollars.

261
00:10:12,663 --> 00:10:14,478
The first of those went to Intel,

262
00:10:14,478 --> 00:10:16,913
at the end of twenty twenty-three.

263
00:10:17,273 --> 00:10:19,475
So the gap between those who have

264
00:10:19,475 --> 00:10:21,676
the machine and those who don't may

265
00:10:21,676 --> 00:10:23,102
keep growing.

266
00:10:26,683 --> 00:10:27,123
So,

267
00:10:27,123 --> 00:10:29,670
the machine only one company can build

268
00:10:29,670 --> 00:10:32,123
is not a single invention.

269
00:10:32,173 --> 00:10:34,058
It is forty years of bets,

270
00:10:34,058 --> 00:10:36,206
a network of suppliers no one else

271
00:10:36,206 --> 00:10:37,137
can copy,

272
00:10:37,137 --> 00:10:39,284
and customers who paid to make it

273
00:10:39,284 --> 00:10:40,123
real.

274
00:10:40,453 --> 00:10:41,958
That is why a small town in

275
00:10:41,958 --> 00:10:43,893
the Netherlands holds one of the most

276
00:10:43,893 --> 00:10:47,073
important keys to the future of computing.

277
00:10:47,423 --> 00:10:49,796
And the smallest details on the most

278
00:10:49,796 --> 00:10:52,433
powerful chips in the world all begin

279
00:10:52,433 --> 00:10:53,989
as a drop of tin,

280
00:10:53,989 --> 00:10:55,544
hit by a laser,

281
00:10:55,544 --> 00:10:57,726
inside one Dutch machine.

282
00:11:02,683 --> 00:11:04,596
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

283
00:11:04,596 --> 00:11:06,879
we follow the money behind another hidden

284
00:11:06,879 --> 00:11:08,833
link of the world economy.

285
00:11:08,833 --> 00:11:11,068
This is The Hidden Economics.
